1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,882
Today, anyone can generate a realistic video in minutes.

2
00:00:04,302 --> 00:00:06,509
It costs almost nothing, and it looks real.

3
00:00:07,509 --> 00:00:08,789
This creates a second problem.

4
00:00:09,209 --> 00:00:12,708
When a video is real, people now say: it is just AI.

5
00:00:13,128 --> 00:00:14,865
True evidence loses its value.

6
00:00:15,865 --> 00:00:19,185
We need a fact from the physical world that a generator does not know.

7
00:00:19,605 --> 00:00:21,802
There is one, and it hangs above your head.

8
00:00:22,222 --> 00:00:23,913
Mains lights flicker with the power grid.

9
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:28,769
The grid frequency drifts a little, all the time, and that drift marks the time.

10
00:00:29,769 --> 00:00:31,169
This problem hurts real people.

11
00:00:31,589 --> 00:00:34,144
Newsrooms must check footage before they publish it.

12
00:00:34,564 --> 00:00:36,642
Courts must decide which video to trust.

13
00:00:37,062 --> 00:00:39,238
Insurers must check claims that use video.

14
00:00:39,658 --> 00:00:43,233
Human-rights groups must defend footage that powerful people call AI.

15
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:47,431
Meet GRIDPRINT. It checks a video against the power grid.

16
00:00:47,851 --> 00:00:50,554
A mains light flickers at two times the grid frequency.

17
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:54,541
On a fifty hertz grid, that is one hundred pulses each second.

18
00:00:55,541 --> 00:00:58,213
A phone camera reads its sensor one row at a time.

19
00:00:58,633 --> 00:00:59,901
This is called a rolling shutter.

20
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:03,689
So the fast flicker becomes faint horizontal bands in the image.

21
00:01:04,689 --> 00:01:07,301
This is a public clip of a real fluorescent light.

22
00:01:07,721 --> 00:01:13,796
OpenCV 5 linearises the light, removes camera motion, and measures the bands in each frame.

23
00:01:14,796 --> 00:01:18,542
From the band phase, the engine rebuilds the grid frequency track.

24
00:01:18,962 --> 00:01:21,443
Then it slides that track along the grid reference log.

25
00:01:21,863 --> 00:01:26,494
One clear match gives a recording time. A cut or an insertion breaks the match.

26
00:01:27,494 --> 00:01:31,284
GRIDPRINT runs live on AWS today. This is the web app.

27
00:01:31,704 --> 00:01:35,764
You upload a video. You can add a claimed grid zone and a claimed recording time.

28
00:01:36,764 --> 00:01:39,953
Ready-made example cases run through the same live pipeline.

29
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:42,321
One click starts a full analysis job.

30
00:01:43,321 --> 00:01:47,573
Here is a finished job. The verdict is VERIFIED, and the report is final.

31
00:01:47,993 --> 00:01:51,500
The page shows the estimated recording time and its uncertainty.

32
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,722
Each verdict comes with reason codes in plain words.

33
00:01:55,722 --> 00:01:57,659
An agent plans each analysis.

34
00:01:58,079 --> 00:02:02,353
It calls engine tools through an MCP server, and it logs every step.

35
00:02:03,353 --> 00:02:06,252
A CONTRADICTED result is never final on its own.

36
00:02:06,672 --> 00:02:08,781
A human reviewer must approve it first.

37
00:02:09,781 --> 00:02:13,295
The limitations page tells users what GRIDPRINT cannot do.

38
00:02:13,715 --> 00:02:15,254
Clear limits are part of the product.

39
00:02:16,254 --> 00:02:19,867
A video arrives through API Gateway and a load balancer.

40
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:27,030
An API service on Fargate stores it in S3 with KMS keys, and puts a job on an SQS queue.

41
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:32,542
Workers on Graviton4 run the OpenCV 5 engine in fifteen stages.

42
00:02:32,962 --> 00:02:40,429
An agent with twelve MCP tools uses Amazon Nova Lite on Bedrock to plan each run, inside strict policies.

43
00:02:40,849 --> 00:02:47,867
DynamoDB keeps jobs and the grid log. IoT Core brings in grid data. Cognito signs in reviewers.

44
00:02:48,867 --> 00:02:52,443
We measured the same engine on Graviton and on x86.

45
00:02:52,863 --> 00:02:57,478
Graviton processed more video per second, at a lower cost per video hour.

46
00:02:58,478 --> 00:03:04,149
GRIDPRINT gives exactly three verdicts: VERIFIED, CONTRADICTED, or INCONCLUSIVE.

47
00:03:04,569 --> 00:03:06,830
A missing signal never counts against a video.

48
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:10,866
It needs mains lighting, and many phone videos will be INCONCLUSIVE.

49
00:03:11,286 --> 00:03:13,837
GRIDPRINT. Evidence from the light in the room.

